from ../LanguageData import DegreeWords, Hedges
from ../meaning/Strengths import Strengths
from ../meaning/Patterns import Patterns

export anchor StatementParts:
    description:
        Once every word has a class, analysis looks for the parts of the
        statement. There are only a few questions to answer.

        ```text
        What is the statement about?              the subject
        What does it say about it?                the verb group
        What value, thing or choice is involved?  the complement
        Is it negated?                            the negation
        How firmly does it bind?                  the modal
        When does it hold?                        the conditions
        How much, or how surely?                  the modifiers
        ```

        These parts are what patterns read, as described in @{Patterns}.

    groups:
        Words gather into groups before they become parts. A noun group is a
        noun with the words that introduce and describe it: the disabled
        button, its action. A verb group is a main verb with the words around
        it that change how it binds: cannot be clicked.

        ```text
        noun group   [determiner or pronoun] [describing words] noun ['s]
                     the disabled button, its action, the button's text

        verb group   [modal] [negation] [forms of be or have] main verb
                     cannot be clicked, is invoked, must not change
        ```

        Groups are found left to right, taking the longest group that fits
        the classes of the words.

    parts:
        With groups found, the parts follow from their order.

        ```text
        subject      the noun group before the verb group
        verb group   the first verb group
        complement   what follows the verb group: a value, a noun group, or
                     alternatives joined by or
        negation     a negation inside the verb group
        modal        a modal inside the verb group
        conditions   clauses opened by a condition word, before or after
        modifiers    degree words and hedges anywhere in the statement
        ```

        The verb group also says whether the subject does the action or has it
        done to it. In the button clicks the link, the button does the
        clicking. In the button is clicked, something else clicks the button.
        A form of be followed by a past participle always means the action is
        done to the subject.

    readingOneStatement:
        ```text
        It cannot be clicked.

        subject      It
        verb group   cannot be clicked      done to the subject
        negation     not, from cannot
        modal        can, from cannot
        complement   (none)
        conditions   (none written here; the property adds one later)
        ```

        ```text
        When clicked, its action is invoked.

        condition    When clicked           no subject of its own
        subject      its action
        verb group   is invoked             done to the subject
        complement   (none)
        ```

    modifiers:
        Degree words from @{DegreeWords}, such as slightly, and hedges from
        @{Hedges}, such as usually, are kept as modifiers rather than dropped.
        A degree word says there is an amount that the statement does not
        give. A hedge makes the statement uncommitted, as described in
        @{Strengths}. Either way, the statement's meaning depends on them, so
        losing them would make it look more precise than it is.

    actionSubjects:
        A subject that starts with a verb ending in -ing, such as clicking
        the button, is an action rather than a thing. No built-in pattern
        accepts an action as a subject, so such statements are unrecognized
        unless a project adds an equivalence rule for them.

    theTerms:
        These ideas have names in linguistics, which are worth knowing when
        reading about the subject elsewhere. A group is usually called a
        phrase, so a noun group is a noun phrase. What this specification
        calls a verb group and complement together are called the predicate:
        everything a statement says about its subject. A clause is a subject
        with its predicate. Working out which word depends on which, as the
        steps above do, is called parsing, and the links found are called
        dependencies. Analysis uses these fixed rules for it rather than a
        general parser, so that every part it finds can be explained.
